<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Grade:</b> 8th Grade (CEFR: A2.3)</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Scenario:</b> Education and Learning</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Theme:</b> This Trimester, I Am Learning About...</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="2"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Text: Summarize audio and video content for study notes. Communication: Facilitate a discussion on team roles in group work.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can summarize key points from an educational video for study notes, using simple visuals and keywords (e.g., drawing a book to represent learning in the video).
Can facilitate a discussion about team roles, using gestures and keywords (e.g., pointing to each team member when assigning roles like, "You research, I write.").
Can summarize audio content for study notes, using gestures to illustrate main ideas (e.g., pointing to the head for "thinking" when discussing study habits).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by displaying images of different school subjects (e.g., history book, math symbols, science lab). Students quickly shout out the subject. Then, the teacher asks, "What subject are you actively learning about this trimester?" and encourages short answers using "I am learning..." This activates prior knowledge of the theme "This Trimester, I Am Learning About..." and prepares students for discussing their learning.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher plays a short 30-second audio clip about effective study habits (e.g., "Active learning involves reading and discussing. You should avoid simply memorizing."). After listening, the teacher models summarizing: "The audio says we should actively learn, not just memorize. I am interested in developing better study skills. It means understanding and discussing." The teacher uses gestures (e.g., pointing to the head for "understanding") and emphasizes stress patterns in "I *am* interested in learning."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I summarized, did I repeat every word from the audio, or just the main ideas?" (Expected answer: "Main ideas.")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What does 'actively learning' mean? Is it just reading silently, or doing more?" (Expected answer: "Doing more, like discussing or taking notes.")</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">[Date] <br> [Time]</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Education and Learning")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short (1.5-2 minute) educational video clip on "Why Education is Important" or "How to Develop Critical Thinking Skills in School." The video should feature clear explanations, simple visuals, and use some of the target vocabulary (e.g., <i>knowledge, skills, grow, achieve, motivated</i>).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students will comprehend the main ideas and key details of an educational video to prepare for summarization, demonstrating their ability to mediate audiovisual content.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question:</b> Before playing the video, the teacher asks, "Watch the video and tell me, what is the main message about education?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> After the first viewing, students listen again and complete a True/False exercise based on key statements from the video (e.g., "Developing skills is important for your future career goals. True/False," "Only memorizing facts helps you grow knowledge. True/False.").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> Students match key vocabulary words (e.g., <i>skills, knowledge, career goals</i>) from the video to their simple definitions or corresponding images provided by the teacher.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">[Date] <br> [Time]</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Mediation)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice summarizing key points from the educational video and using gestures to illustrate main ideas, applying present continuous for ongoing actions, gerunds after prepositions, and modals for desires/ability, alongside target vocabulary (<i>education, skills, knowledge, learn, study, develop, grow, achieve</i>). Pragmatic functions like discussing goals and offering encouragement will be integrated.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Keyword Summary (Controlled Practice):</b> In pairs, students receive a worksheet with blanks corresponding to a summary of the video. They fill in the blanks using keywords and phrases from the video and the target vocabulary. For example: "The video is about the importance of _______. We are ______ new skills. Many students are interested in _______ for their future ______ goals. I would like to _______ more." The teacher encourages them to draw simple visuals (e.g., a lightbulb for "knowledge," an upward arrow for "grow") next to keywords to represent the ideas.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Gesture & Rephrase (Semi-controlled):</b> Students work in small groups. The teacher provides 3-4 key sentences from the video's content. Each group must rephrase each sentence into a simpler form and prepare a gesture to illustrate its main idea. For example, original sentence: "Lifelong learning is crucial for developing your critical thinking skills." Group rephrased: "Learning all your life helps you think well." Gesture: Point to head and then show a growing motion with hands. The teacher reminds them to use present continuous and modals where applicable, e.g., "We are trying to develop new ways of explaining."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Peer Explanation (Pair Interaction):</b> Students pair up. Each student takes turns explaining one or two key messages from the video to their partner, without looking at their notes, but using the gestures they practiced. Their partner listens and gives feedback on clarity and effectiveness of gestures, using pragmatic functions like "That was clear!" or "You should keep practicing that gesture for 'knowledge'." The teacher circulates, providing support and correcting pronunciation, especially stress patterns in "I *am* learning" or "I *would* like to study."</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">[Date] <br> [Time]</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Education and Learning")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative task:</b> Students will work in their project groups (from the "Educational Presentation" 21st Century Skills Project) to facilitate a discussion about their individual contributions, summarizing their part of the research and proposing team roles for finalizing the presentation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task format:</b> Small group discussion and role assignment. Each student will present a brief oral summary of the information they gathered for their part of the "Educational Presentation" on the importance of education. They must use present continuous to describe ongoing learning related to their research (e.g., "This trimester, I am learning a lot about different career goals") and modals to express future contributions or desires (e.g., "I would like to create the slides for our presentation," "We should all review the script."). As a group, they will then facilitate a discussion to assign specific roles for the final presentation, using gestures to assign roles (e.g., pointing to a classmate and saying "You research, I write").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme/Scenario:</b> The task directly involves discussing the "Educational Presentation" project, which centers on the importance of education. Students mediate their research findings and facilitate group work related to the project, demonstrating practical application of the theme "This Trimester, I Am Learning About..." and the scenario "Education and Learning." They engage in discussions about personal and learning goals and offer relevant suggestions for improvement.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">[Date] <br> [Time]</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist and Peer Assessment (for group interaction). The teacher will use a checklist to evaluate individual students' ability to summarize effectively and facilitate discussion, while peers will assess their group members' participation and clarity.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each student, within their project group, will: 1) Deliver a 1-minute oral summary of their research contribution to the "Educational Presentation," highlighting 2-3 key points using visuals/keywords and gestures. 2) Actively participate in facilitating the discussion on team roles for the final presentation, offering suggestions and assigning tasks using gestures and target language.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity of Summary:</b> Can effectively summarize key points from their research.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Keywords & Gestures:</b> Employs relevant vocabulary and simple visuals/gestures to enhance understanding.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar & Pronunciation:</b> Uses present continuous and modals accurately; pronunciation is intelligible with appropriate stress patterns (e.g., "I *am* learning," "I *would* like").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Facilitation of Discussion:</b> Contributes to group discussion about roles, offers suggestions, and respects others' opinions.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two sentences:
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Today, I learned that summarizing helps me _______."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One thing I still need to practice when facilitating group discussions is _______."</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">[Date] <br> [Time]</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did using gestures help you to summarize or explain your ideas to your classmates?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of facilitating the discussion on team roles did you find most challenging, and why?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The mediation activities were effective in helping students summarize complex information into manageable notes and facilitated discussions. The use of gestures to illustrate ideas was well-received and helped reinforce meaning for A2.3 learners. Some students still need more practice with precise articulation of present continuous and gerund forms under pressure. I will need to provide more scaffolded practice in upcoming lessons focusing on clear, concise summaries and explicit guidance on leading group discussions, perhaps with sentence starters for facilitating. Emphasizing minimal pair sounds like /ɪ/ in "skill" vs /iː/ in "skull" during feedback was helpful but could be more integrated into a dedicated pronunciation moment if needed.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson concludes the theme by having students mediate and integrate their learning for the "Educational Presentation." The next theme will build on these collaborative skills, allowing students to apply their developed ability to summarize, facilitate, and present information in a new context, possibly involving more complex academic discussions or project planning.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">[Date] <br> [Time]</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>